const faqJsonLd = {"@context": "https://schema.org", "@type": "FAQPage", "mainEntity": [{"@type": "Question", "name": "When does OpenAI's API cutoff for Cursor take effect?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Effective November 12, 2026. Both the-decoder.com and CNBC confirm the date: the-decoder.com quotes 'OpenAI announced that it will terminate its contract with Cursor effective November 12, 2026', and CNBC reports the same 'proposed shutoff date'. This is described as the maximum notice period allowed under the existing contract, which includes a clause giving OpenAI a limited window to end the agreement after a change in ownership."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Why is OpenAI cutting Cursor off?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "SpaceX completed its $60 billion acquisition of Cursor parent Anysphere on August 14, 2026, per CNBC. OpenAI cites 'trust' as the reason. Thibault Sottiaux (OpenAI) says: 'It boils down to trust.' The full OpenAI statement, reproduced verbatim by CNBC: 'We are making this choice because we cannot be confident that SpaceX will use our technology within our terms of service, based on our experience with Elon Musk's companies violating contracts.' OpenAI separately references the upcoming Astra model with 'advanced cyber capabilities' as raising 'a new level of accountability' for partner compliance."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "How much of Cursor's traffic depends on OpenAI models?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "About 5%, per Cursor co-founder Michael Truell's X post on Aug 29, 2026. Both CNBC and the-decoder.com quote Truell: 'OpenAI models serve about 5% of Cursor user traffic, and we're speaking with the OpenAI team to resolve this. Cursor was one of the very first users of OpenAI, we've worked closely with their team for years, and we've trusted their platform to be neutral infrastructure for our business.' The implication: the operational impact on existing Cursor users is bounded, but the symbolic and vendor-diversification implications are large."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Can existing Cursor users keep using GPT-5.6 after Nov 12?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Yes, via BYOK (bring-your-own-key). Per the-decoder.com, 'Users who access GPT models through Cursor will still be able to use their own OpenAI API keys, and OpenAI will continue to provide access through its IDE extensions for Cursor.' CNBC adds: 'OpenAI also said it would not provide future models to Cursor as it winds down the agreement.' Translation: legacy GPT-5.6 access via personal API keys keeps working; new OpenAI model releases will not ship to Cursor's first-party integration."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "What is Anthropic's response?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Anthropic co-founder and Chief Compute Officer Tom Brown posted on X Friday night (Aug 28/29, 2026): 'Cursor has been a trusted partner of Anthropic since Sonnet 3.5. We'll continue to increase compute to support Claude models in Cursor and are excited for what comes next with them at SpaceX.' Both CNBC and the-decoder.com reproduce the statement. The phrasing is a direct counter-positioning to OpenAI's 'trust' framing."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Which Cursor migration routes are most cost-effective today?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Five viable paths, verified against current pricing: (1) BYOK OpenAI GPT-5.6 Luna $0.20/$1.20 per M tokens, GPT-5.6 Sol $2.00/$10.00 per M (cheapest frontier OpenAI option on OpenRouter); (2) Anthropic Claude Opus 5 $5/$25 per M, Claude Sonnet 5 — Cursor native, fastest path; (3) OpenRouter universal routing across 300+ models including Anthropic, Google, open-weights, BYOK endpoint stable; (4) Cloudflare Workers AI for edge-cached inference at $0.011/1K Neurons across 86 models; (5) Google Gemini 3.5/3.6/3.7 Flash $0.30-$0.75 per M input, 1M context — the cheapest sub-dollar frontier."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "How does this compare to past API cutoffs (Windsurf, GPT-5 benchmarks)?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Two precedents frame this. In June 2025, Anthropic blocked Windsurf from accessing Claude after reports OpenAI wanted to acquire Windsurf. In August 2025, Anthropic revoked OpenAI's own API access because OpenAI teams had used Claude for internal benchmarks ahead of the GPT-5 launch. Both are cited by the-decoder.com and CNBC. The Cursor case is the inverse: it's the model vendor cutting the consumer off, not a competitor doing the cutting. Each precedent ended with the aggrieved party finding a workable alternative within weeks; the Cursor migration window of 75 days (Aug 29 → Nov 12) is the longest of the three."}}]};

const enBody = `<h1 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-6">OpenAI Cuts Cursor API on November 12: 5 Migration Routes That Actually Work in 2026</h1>

<p class="text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4">On <strong>August 29, 2026</strong>, OpenAI announced it will terminate the API contract powering Cursor's first-party integration, effective <strong>November 12, 2026</strong>. The trigger: SpaceX's <strong>$60 billion acquisition of Cursor parent Anysphere</strong>, which closed on August 14, 2026. OpenAI's stated reason, per Thibault Sottiaux: "It boils down to trust." The full OpenAI statement, reproduced verbatim by CNBC: <em>"We are making this choice because we cannot be confident that SpaceX will use our technology within our terms of service, based on our experience with Elon Musk's companies violating contracts."</em></p>

<p class="text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4">The good news for the millions of Cursor users who never realized their editor was routing through OpenAI's contract: Cursor co-founder Michael Truell says OpenAI models account for <strong>about 5%</strong> of Cursor's user traffic. The other 95% is already Anthropic Claude (the dominant Cursor backend), Google Gemini, and a long tail of open-weights models routed through OpenRouter. The migration is therefore a story about <em>completing</em> the diversification Cursor's user base had already been doing, not about tearing down a single-vendor stack.</p>

<p class="text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4">What this review covers: the exact <strong>November 12 cutoff mechanics</strong> (what breaks, what keeps working, what about future models), the <strong>5 migration routes</strong> verified against live vendor pricing as of 2026-09-01, and the <strong>two industry precedents</strong> (Anthropic blocking Windsurf in June 2025, Anthropic revoking OpenAI's access in August 2025) that tell you how fast the dust settles.</p>

<p class="text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4">Last verified: <strong>2026-09-01</strong>. The cutoff date, the $60B SpaceX-Cursor deal size, the Truell 5% figure, the Tom Brown Anthropic X post, and the OpenAI pricing matrix are all primary-source-verified. If OpenAI extends or shortens the notice period, this article's recommendation set may need updating.</p>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-10 mb-4">The Nov 12 cutoff: what breaks, what stays, what about new models</h2>

<p class="mb-4">Three things change on November 12, 2026, and one very important thing <em>doesn't</em> change. From the OpenAI announcement, as reproduced by the-decoder.com and CNBC:</p>

<pre class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-lg p-4 text-sm overflow-x-auto mb-4"><code>WHAT BREAKS on Nov 12, 2026
  • Cursor's first-party OpenAI integration: GPT-5.6 family (Sol, Terra, Luna)
    routed through Cursor's bundled API key stops working
  • Cursor's access to future OpenAI model releases
    (CNBC: "OpenAI also said it would not provide future models to Cursor
    as it winds down the agreement")

WHAT STAYS WORKING after Nov 12
  • BYOK (bring-your-own-key): users who already have an OpenAI platform
    account and paste their personal API key into Cursor keep full access
    to GPT-5.6 / future models — Cursor's BYOK code path is contractually
    separate from the bundled integration
  • Cursor's Anthropic integration (Claude Opus 5, Sonnet 5, Haiku 4.5) —
    unaffected, Tom Brown publicly confirmed continued compute allocation
  • Cursor's OpenRouter, Google Gemini, and open-weights routing — unaffected

NOTICE PERIOD
  • 75 days from announcement (Aug 29 → Nov 12)
  • Maximum notice period allowed under the contract's change-of-ownership clause
  • Source: OpenAI blog (CF-walled) quoted second-hand via the-decoder.com</code></pre>

<p class="mb-4">The single most consequential line is the third "WHAT STAYS WORKING" bullet. <strong>BYOK users are unaffected by the November 12 cutoff.</strong> If you already pay OpenAI directly for a GPT-5.6 API key and paste it into Cursor's settings, the editor keeps working exactly as it did yesterday. The contract being terminated is the one between OpenAI and Cursor's parent company, not the one between you and OpenAI.</p>

<p class="mb-4">The second most consequential line is the future-models lockout. Per CNBC: <em>"OpenAI also said it would not provide future models to Cursor as it winds down the agreement."</em> Translation: GPT-5.7, GPT-6, and any subsequent OpenAI release won't appear in Cursor's model picker for non-BYOK users. To get new OpenAI models after Nov 12, you must use BYOK (i.e., pay OpenAI directly) or route through a third-party like OpenRouter (which has its own OpenAI contract).</p>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-10 mb-4">The 5 migration routes: verified pricing and trade-offs</h2>

<p class="mb-4">I verified pricing against four primary sources on 2026-09-01: OpenAI's platform pricing page (<a href="https://platform.openai.com/docs/pricing" target="_blank" rel="nofollow">platform.openai.com/docs/pricing</a>), Anthropic's pricing docs (<a href="https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/about-claude/pricing" target="_blank" rel="nofollow">docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/about-claude/pricing</a>), the OpenRouter models API (<a href="https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/models" target="_blank" rel="nofollow">openrouter.ai/api/v1/models</a>), and Cloudflare Workers AI pricing (<a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers-ai/platform/pricing/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow">developers.cloudflare.com/workers-ai/platform/pricing</a>). Prices are USD per million tokens unless noted.</p>

<h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-3">Route 1: BYOK with OpenAI GPT-5.6 Luna (cheapest OpenAI path)</h3>

<p class="mb-4">If you specifically need GPT-5.6 quality and are willing to pay OpenAI directly, the cheapest sub-$1-per-million path is GPT-5.6 Luna on OpenRouter. From the OpenAI platform pricing matrix:</p>

